      "writtenOn": "2026-08-18T12:30:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "And it doesn\u0027t seem to work. The complement notation `^~(.*)$` is deprecated in Lucene, but still enabled in Gerrit. Using it alone provides the expected results. If you combine it with the non-complement regex (`path:\"^uv\\.lock$\" -path:\"^~(uv\\.lock)$\"`), the resulting set is not the expected one, but the same as if only the non-complemented version is being used. The reason is the following:\n\nA Lucene document stores each path as a separate key-value pair. Matching just checks if any of the entries match, which doesn\u0027t match the desired behaviour. As examples let\u0027s consider the following changes:\n\n```\n- change_1:\n    files:\n      - uv.lock\n- change_2:\n    files:\n      - uv.lock\n      - README.md\n- change_2:\n    files:\n      - README.md\n```\n\nThe desired behaviour is that the query `onlypaths:uv.lock` only returns change_1.\n\n`path:uv.lock` returns change_1 and change_2 as expected.\n`path:uv.lock -path:\"^~(uv\\.lock)$\"` which should be equivalent of `onlypaths:` according to Sasa\u0027s suggestion still returns change_1 and change_2. For change_2 it matches because the first term matches the file `uv.lock` and the second term matches the file `uv.lock` as well. Since both terms match at leas tone value, Lucene considers it a match. As far as I was able to find, Lucene does not provide a \"all fields of a type must match\" functionality. Thus, we always need post processing.",
      "parentUuid": "d5fff7cc_5279505a",
